{
  "format": "wildlands-process",
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "revision": 2,
  "id": "delivery-release",
  "name": "Weekly delivery and release train",
  "description": "A product team's delivery process: a weekly cadence of backlog refinement, iteration planning, daily stand-ups, review and retrospective feeds a release train that ships a small increment every week. One case is one product, followed from the approved idea through an inception and a Hello World walking skeleton released as 0.1.0, then weekly iterations, each released as the next minor version 0.x.0 (the increments counter is the minor version), until the MVP scope is released and launched as 1.0.0. The MVP scope (6 to 8 increments) is drawn when the product arrives, and in 30% of reviews stakeholder feedback adds one increment to it. Planning commits 3 to 5 items; developers build them in parallel (multi-instance: a fourth or fifth item queues for one of three developers) while four iteration days, each opening with a daily stand-up, run one after another (sequential multi-instance). An item still in progress after three days escalates to the daily stand-up (a non-interrupting deadline) while the work continues. Before each release an inclusive gateway adds a UX acceptance when the user interface changed (60%), a data-migration rehearsal when stored data changed (25%), both, or neither. CI and the release pipeline are automated system steps that execute nothing. Time is in business minutes, 480 per working day, so an iteration takes about one working week. Every random value derives from the definition seed 7 and the case and step, so the same seed always gives the same run. All durations, costs, capacities and probabilities are synthetic illustrative assumptions, not measured team performance or a forecast.",
  "seed": 7,
  "start": "idea",
